Electrical discharge machining of Hastelloy alloys is sensitive to the electrode material and its microstructure: studies that specifically investigate graphite electrodes report clear links between graphite grain size, process efficiency, tool wear and surface finish. Nowicki et al. (2022) compared POCO graphites with grain sizes of 1 µm (AF-5) and 10 µm (S-180) and found that coarser-grained graphite yields higher material removal rate (MRR) but also greater relative tool wear, whereas the finer-grained AF-5 produced the lowest surface roughness (Ra). {{cite:S1#c1}} The later work by the same group extends these observations for EDM with negative polarity and reports that fine-grained graphite increases process efficiency while reducing tool wear; the authors link detached graphite grains to instability in discharge behaviour and altered removal mechanisms, and they provide mathematical models to predict MRR, relative tool wear rate (TWR) and Ra from machining parameters. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Across the reviewed EDM studies, pulse energy metrics—principally discharge current (peak current) and pulse duration—emerge repeatedly as the dominant factors controlling both MRR and surface roughness. Nowicki et al. identify discharge current and pulse duration as the main drivers of MRR and Ra for both graphite grades, and they find the time interval (pulse off-time) most influential for relative graphite electrode wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} Complementary experimental designs using Taguchi methods show peak current to be the single most influential factor on MRR and Ra in cryogenically treated superalloy workpieces, with ANOVA attributing 86.43% and 74.79% influence of peak current on MRR and Ra respectively in that study. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Work that examines geometric and stability consequences of electrode wear reinforces the importance of current and pulse duration for maintaining electrode shape during EDM of Hastelloy variants: Ramabalan et al. found that higher current and longer pulses strongly affect electrode taper, wear length and edge degradation, causing originally sharp electrode corners to become hemispherical under unstable sparking and heat diffusion. {{cite:S4#c1}} Broad reviews and surveys of EDM process enhancements report a range of auxiliary approaches—cryogenic treatment, powder-mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance, and others—that aim to reduce TWR and improve MRR and surface integrity. {{cite:S6#c1}} Studies on nanopowder-mixed dielectrics coupled with electrode cryogenic treatment indicate substantial reductions in electrode wear and overcut for brass electrodes in other materials, demonstrating the potential of combined process modifications to improve dimensional accuracy and EWR. {{cite:S7#c1}}

Electrical discharge coating (EDC) and related surface-modification techniques are also discussed in the literature as routes to modify surface properties via discharge processes, but information connecting these coating approaches directly to electrode selection for Hastelloy EDM is not provided in the available sources. {{cite:S5#c1}} 

