Additively manufactured 18Ni-300 (maraging) steels produced by selective laser melting (SLM) exhibit distinctly fine, rapidly solidified microstructures in the as-built condition, with ultrafine submicron grains and martensitic matrix that reflect very high cooling rates during layerwise melting and solidification. {{cite:S3#c1}} SLM parts often reach near-full density when parameters are optimised, which permits production of functional components with limited porosity. {{cite:S1#c1}} The SLM process therefore imposes an intrinsic thermal history that both refines grains and promotes in situ tempering and precipitation phenomena not typically present in conventionally manufactured counterparts. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Electron microscopy studies report abundant nanoscale needle-shaped precipitates (Ni3X, X = Ti, Al, Mo) dispersed within the martensitic matrix after standard SLM fabrication, and these precipitates are linked to the material’s age-hardening response. {{cite:S3#c1}} In one detailed microstructural survey, the as-fabricated microstructure is described as fine-grained martensite with no strong crystallographic texture across build orientations, and an as-printed surface roughness around $Ra\approx 10\ \mu$m prior to any surface finishing. {{cite:S8#c1}} The hierarchical (sub)grain structures and fine precipitates induced by AM correlate with enhanced strength and wear resistance relative to conventional materials in several reviews. {{cite:S6#c1}} 

Thermal treatments alter these SLM microstructures by promoting precipitate evolution, stress relief and, under particular intercritical regimes, partial reversion of martensite to austenite. {{cite:S3#c1}} Studies that apply solution treatment, aging or combinations including plasma nitriding at temperatures near typical aging cycles document microstructural dependence of the thermochemical layer morphology and of mechanical and corrosion-related properties. {{cite:S2#c1}} Optimised aging produces maximum nano-hardness and is associated with precipitation-controlled hardness; aging also shifts corrosion potentials in heat-treated material. {{cite:S8#c1}} Plasma nitriding performed without prior aging can produce defects in the compound layer, whereas nitriding applied after prior heat treatments improves wear and corrosion resistance of SLM maraging steel. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Reversed (reverted) austenite formed by tempering in the intercritical $\alpha' + \gamma$ range has a clear effect on localized corrosion performance in chloride media. {{cite:S4#c1}} An intercritical temper at $720^\circ\mathrm{C}$ produced reversed austenite at martensite lath boundaries, driven by local Ni enrichment from intermetallic dissolution, and the specimens with this reversed austenite showed higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density and reduced corrosion rate in $3.5\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ NaCl compared with as-printed, aged, or homogenised samples lacking reverted austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}} General corrosion and tribological testing across as-built and heat-treated SLM maraging steels have been characterised and compared to wrought counterparts, indicating that despite finer microstructure, corrosion behaviour in heat-treated conditions can be similar to conventional material and that precipitation phenomena play a central role in both hardness and corrosion response. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: quantitative comparisons of pitting morphology and pit chemistry]]

Additively manufactured 18Ni-300 maraging steel produced by selective laser melting (SLM) develops a distinctly fine microstructure as a direct consequence of the process's high cooling rates and layer-wise thermal history. Electron microscopy on optimised SLM builds reports an average submicron grain size of $0.31\ \mu$m, consistent with cooling on the order of $10^{7}\ \mathrm{K/s}$, and a martensitic matrix containing dense, needle-shaped nanoprecipitates of Ni$_3$X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) that underpin age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Scanning-electron-based mapping across multiple build sections likewise finds a fine martensitic structure in the as-printed state with no pronounced crystallographic texture and a high as-built surface roughness (Ra ≈ 10 µm). {{cite:S8#c1}} Reviews of AM steels emphasise that these hierarchical subgrain structures and fine precipitates are characteristic of laser powder bed fusion and are linked to enhanced strength and wear resistance relative to conventional counterparts. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Conventional heat treatments and intercritical tempering modify these as-built features in reproducible ways. Systematic heat-treatment studies show that aging produces the expected precipitation of Ni$_3$X phases inside the martensitic matrix, which increases hardness through mechanisms described using Orowan bowing and coherency strain hardening, and that heat treatment relieves residual stresses and tends to reduce build-direction mechanical anisotropy. {{cite:S3#c1}} Thermo-chemical treatments applied after different prior heat treatments produce different surface-layer responses: for example, plasma nitriding performed without prior aging induces cracks in the compound nitride layer, whereas nitriding after prior heat treatment avoids those defects and improves wear and corrosion resistance compared with untreated AM material. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Austenite reversion is one specific heat-treatment outcome that has been linked to altered corrosion behaviour. Intercritical tempering within the mixed $\alpha' + \gamma$ range at about $720^\circ$C produced reverted austenite that nucleates mainly along martensite-lath boundaries, promoted by local Ni enrichment from dissolution of intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} In the study that tested pitting in a 3.5 wt% NaCl electrolyte, samples tempered to produce reversed austenite exhibited a more positive pitting potential, a lower corrosion current density, and a lower corrosion rate than as-printed, conventionally aged, or homogenized specimens lacking reverted austenite, leading the authors to conclude that reversed austenite increases pitting resistance in maraging steel. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Surface condition and post-processing remain important modifiers of corrosion performance. Comparisons between SLM and wrought maraging steels show that when both are subjected to comparable heat treatments their nano-hardness and corrosion behaviour can be similar, and that electropolishing of SLM surfaces markedly reduces roughness (Ra reduced by ~65%), a process that the authors link to modified corrosion response. {{cite:S8#c1}} Taken together, the literature shows consistent microstructural signatures from SLM, predictable effects of standard heat treatments including austenite reversion, and limited but direct evidence that reverted austenite can improve resistance to chloride-induced pitting. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S4#c1}}

